I have enough authoritative material. The PTX ISA detail pages don't render well via WebFetch (single-page TOC), but the CUTLASS source `cvt.rn.tf32.f32` instruction and the cuBLAS docs are the authoritative, citable anchors. Let me do two final targeted fetches: the A100 whitepaper / TensorRT accuracy page for the round-trip statement, and verify the round_half_ulp_truncate semantics one more time via the float-conversion-back-to-float operator. Both findings are already strong; this is corroboration.

I have everything needed. Let me compile the final report.

---

# FP32 → TF32 conversion: rounding mode in CUTLASS and cuBLAS

## Bottom line first

**The conventional, documented FP32→TF32 reduction is ROUND-TO-NEAREST, not a plain truncation.** Both the hardware PTX instruction CUTLASS emits (`cvt.rn.tf32.f32`) and cuBLAS's documentation state round-to-nearest-even. However, there is an important subtlety: **CUTLASS's *default* path for the `tfloat32_t` type and for GEMM is `round_half_ulp_truncate`** — a "round half up then drop the low bits" scheme (add `0x1000` then truncate the low 13 bits), which is a *rounding* approximation, **not** a pure round-toward-zero truncation. A genuine bit-dropping truncation exists too (`round_toward_zero`, mask `& 0xffffe000`) but is not the default. So "truncation vs round-to-nearest" is not a clean binary in CUTLASS — there are four distinct converters, and the one you get depends on which API you call.

---

## 1. CUTLASS

### 1a. The `tfloat32_t` type and its `float` constructor — default is `round_half_ulp_truncate`

File: `include/cutlass/tfloat32.h`

The struct is `struct alignas(4) tfloat32_t { uint32_t storage; }`. Its `float` constructor (lines 106-108) does **not** use round-to-nearest; it uses `round_half_ulp_truncate`:

```cpp
// tfloat32.h:106-108
/// Floating-point conversion - round toward nearest even
CUTLASS_HOST_DEVICE
explicit tfloat32_t(float x): storage(round_half_ulp_truncate(x).raw()) { }
```

(The comment says "round toward nearest even" but the implementation it calls is the half-ulp-truncate approximation — the comment is aspirational, not literal.)

`round_half_ulp_truncate` (lines 86-102) adds `0x1000u` (half a ulp at the tf32 boundary) to the FP32 bit pattern of finite values, then bitcasts — it does **not** explicitly mask off the low bits:

```cpp
// tfloat32.h:86-102
/// Emulated rounding is fast in device code
CUTLASS_HOST_DEVICE
static tfloat32_t round_half_ulp_truncate(float const &s) {
  uint32_t x = float_to_storage(s);

  #if defined(__CUDA_ARCH__)
  if (::isfinite(s)) {
    x += 0x1000u;
  }
  #else
  if (std::isfinite(s)) {
    x += 0x1000u;
  }
  #endif

  return tfloat32_t::bitcast(x);
}
```

The low 13 "don't-care" bits are only cleared lazily when converting *back* to `float` (lines 125-131):

```cpp
// tfloat32.h:125-131
// Conversion to float
CUTLASS_HOST_DEVICE
operator float() const {
  // Conversions to IEEE single-precision requires clearing dont-care bits
  // of the mantissa.
  unsigned bits = (storage & ~0x1fffu);
```

`numeric_limits<tfloat32_t>::round_style` is declared `std::round_to_nearest` (line 296), but as the code shows, the actual default constructor path is the half-ulp-truncate approximation.

### 1b. `NumericConverter<tfloat32_t, float>` — four converters; template default vs PreferredRoundingMode default

File: `include/cutlass/numeric_conversion.h`

The base `NumericConverter` template default is round-to-nearest (line 69-73):

```cpp
// numeric_conversion.h:69-73
template <
  typename T,
  typename S,
  FloatRoundStyle Round = FloatRoundStyle::round_to_nearest
>
struct NumericConverter {
```

There are **four** explicit `float → tfloat32_t` specializations:

1. **`round_to_nearest`** (lines 628-676) — this is the only one that uses the **hardware instruction** on Hopper+ and a correct round-to-nearest-even software fallback elsewhere:
   ```cpp
   // numeric_conversion.h:639-650
   #if defined(__CUDA_ARCH__) && __CUDA_ARCH__ >= 900
       asm volatile("cvt.rn.tf32.f32 %0, %1;" : "=r"(storage) : "r"(storage));
   #else
       if ((storage & 0x7f800000) != 0x7f800000) {
         bool mantissa_bit = ((storage & (1 << 13)) != 0);
         bool round_bit = ((storage & (1 << 12)) != 0);
         bool sticky_bit = ((storage & ((1 << 12) - 1)) != 0);
         if ((round_bit && sticky_bit) || (round_bit && mantissa_bit)) {
           storage += uint32_t(1 << 13);
         }
   ```
   The comment here is the key statement about why the low bits are *not* forcibly truncated (lines 652-662):
   ```cpp
   // Note, the following is intentionally commented out. TF32
   // does not define the low order bits, so they may be left in
   // an undefined state.
   //
   // By not truncating these bit explicitly, we avoid an extra logical
   // operation.
   ```

2. **`round_half_ulp_truncate`** (lines 678-693) — delegates to `tfloat32_t::round_half_ulp_truncate` (the add-`0x1000` scheme above).

3. **`round_half_ulp_trunc_dntz`** (lines 697-718) — same idea but rounds denormals toward zero, branch-free.

4. **`round_toward_zero`** (lines 720-736) — this is the *only* genuine bit-dropping truncation: `bitcast(x & 0xffffe000)`.

**The default rounding the GEMM machinery actually uses** is set by `PreferredRoundingMode` (lines 8001-8013):

```cpp
// numeric_conversion.h:8001-8013
/// Defines preferred rounding mode for a pair of types
template <typename T, typename S>
struct PreferredRoundingMode {
  static FloatRoundStyle const kRound = FloatRoundStyle::round_to_nearest;
};

#if defined(__CUDA_ARCH__) && __CUDA_ARCH__ < 900
/// Defines preferred rounding mode for a pair of types
template <>
struct PreferredRoundingMode<cutlass::tfloat32_t, float> {
  static FloatRoundStyle const kRound = FloatRoundStyle::round_half_ulp_truncate;
};
#endif
```

So: on **pre-Hopper (sm < 90)** the preferred default for `tfloat32_t`-from-`float` is **`round_half_ulp_truncate`** (the cheap emulated rounding, since there was no `cvt.rn.tf32.f32` instruction before sm_90 — sm_80 only had `cvt.rna.tf32.f32`). On **Hopper+ (sm ≥ 90)** the generic `round_to_nearest` is preferred, which lowers to the hardware `cvt.rn.tf32.f32`. The `FloatRoundStyle` enum documents the modes (lines 60-64):
```cpp
round_half_ulp_truncate,      ///< add 0.5ulp to integer representation then round toward zero
round_half_ulp_trunc_dntz     ///< like round_half_ulp_truncate, except denorms are rounded *toward* zero
```

### 1c. 3xTF32 / TF32x3 / "FastF32" — splitting one FP32 into multiple TF32 values

Yes — this is real and it is exactly an FP32-split scheme. The implementation is `NumericConverterFastF32` (lines 743-774):

```cpp
// numeric_conversion.h:743-773
template <
  FloatRoundStyle RoundBig = FloatRoundStyle::round_toward_zero,
  FloatRoundStyle RoundSmall = FloatRoundStyle::round_half_ulp_truncate
>
struct NumericConverterFastF32 {
  // result_type holds big cutlass::tfloat32_t at idx(0) and small cutlass::tfloat32_t at idx(1)
  using result_type = Array<cutlass::tfloat32_t, 2>;
  ...
    result[0] = convert_big_(source);
    result[1] = convert_small_(source - static_cast<float>(result[0]));
```

It decomposes each FP32 into a "big" TF32 plus a "small" TF32 capturing the residual (`source - big`). Doing the GEMM with these components and summing the partial products recovers near-FP32 accuracy. Note the per-component defaults: **big** uses `round_toward_zero` (true truncation) and **small** uses `round_half_ulp_truncate`. CUTLASS markets this as "3xTF32"/"TF32x3" (3 TF32 MMA passes: big×big, big×small, small×big) and ships the `examples/27_ampere_3xtf32_fast_accurate_tensorop_gemm` sample; it is the standard way CUTLASS emulates full FP32 precision on TF32 tensor cores.

---

## 2. cuBLAS — `CUBLAS_COMPUTE_32F_FAST_TF32`

Source: cuBLAS documentation, https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cublas/index.html (cuBLAS 13.3).

The docs are explicit and state **round-to-nearest-even**, not truncation:

- `cublasComputeType_t` → **`CUBLAS_COMPUTE_32F_FAST_TF32`**:
  > "Allows the library to use Tensor Cores with TF32 compute for 32-bit floating point input and output matrices. **Note that input conversions round to nearest even.**"

- `cublasMath_t` → **`CUBLAS_TF32_TENSOR_OP_MATH`**:
  > "Enable acceleration of single-precision routines using TF32 tensor cores. **Note that input conversions round to nearest even.**"

The docs also note that `NVIDIA_TF32_OVERRIDE=0` disables TF32 acceleration library-wide. (cuBLAS does not, in these sections, add an explicit numerical-bias warning beyond stating the round-to-nearest-even conversion and the reduced TF32 precision.)

---

## 3. CUDA C Programming Guide / PTX ISA — hardware rounding

The authoritative hardware statement lives in the **PTX ISA**, in the `cvt` instruction (Data Movement and Conversion Instructions). The instruction CUTLASS emits, `cvt.rn.tf32.f32`, carries the `.rn` modifier = **round-to-nearest, ties-to-even** (IEEE 754 default). PTX describes the tf32 destination as "the same range as `.f32` and reduced precision (≥10 bits of mantissa)," requiring a `.b32` register. The supported rounding modifiers for the tf32 destination are:
